## 3. Architectural principle

Use the dumbbell program as the structural base because dumbbells provide the clearest home strength anchors.

Use bands only when they improve one of these roles:

* true vertical pulling with confirmed high anchor,
* horizontal pulling,
* anti-rotation,
* scapular or rear-delt reinforcement,
* resistance-profile variety,
* safe pain-aware substitution,
* or a useful exercise not efficiently supplied by dumbbells.

Do not use bands merely because the user selected them.

Every band exercise must justify why it is better than the available dumbbell or bodyweight alternative for that slot.

The final program should feel like one coherent system, not two programs merged together.

## 4. Capability-specific mixed-home lanes

Support at minimum:

### Lane A — Dumbbells + long band with secure repositionable anchor

May use:

* dumbbell strength anchors,
* true high-anchor vertical pulling,
* middle-anchor rows or presses,
* low-anchor work,
* anti-rotation,
* and mini-loop work when also confirmed.

Expected advantage:

* the most complete mixed-home movement coverage,
* including true vertical pulling where safe.

### Lane B — Dumbbells + long band without anchor

May use:

* dumbbell strength anchors,
* self-anchored band work,
* band-under-foot patterns,
* safe band rows around the feet where valid,
* and bodyweight support work.

Must not use:

* pulldowns,
* anchored chest press,
* anchored row,
* face pull requiring a fixed anchor,
* Pallof press requiring an anchor,
* or any unconfirmed anchor exercise.

True vertical pulling remains unavailable unless a pull-up bar is independently confirmed.

### Lane C — Dumbbells + mini loops only

Use:

* dumbbells for primary upper- and lower-body strength,
* mini loops for lateral hip, selected lower-body, shoulder or scapular reinforcement,
* bodyweight trunk work.

Must not schedule:

* long-band rows,
* long-band presses,
* pulldowns,
* anchored anti-rotation,
* or long-band hinges.

Mini loops must not create false pulling coverage.

### Lane D — Dumbbells + both band types

Use the best legal combination based on anchor capability.

Do not assume the long band has an anchor.

### Optional capability lane

Where a pull-up bar is explicitly confirmed, true vertical pulling may be supplied through pull-ups or approved progressions while preserving mixed-home identity.

## 5. Canonical three-day architecture

Use recognizable Full Body A/B/C identities that align with the dumbbell foundation.

### Full Body A — Squat, Press and Row

Required primary roles:

* knee-dominant dumbbell or appropriate loaded squat,
* horizontal press,
* true horizontal pull.

Band use may improve:

* horizontal pulling,
* scapular reinforcement,
* or trunk stability.

Do not replace a clear dumbbell strength anchor with a lower-value band movement without documented reason.

### Full Body B — Hinge, Overhead and Unilateral

Required primary roles:

* true hinge,
* vertical press when safe,
* unilateral lower-body pattern.

Required support:

* pulling or scapular reinforcement,
* anti-rotation or lateral stability.

Bands may supply anti-rotation only when anchor capability is confirmed.

The primary hinge must remain truthful and may not degrade into curls, pulses, preparation drills or unrelated unilateral work.

### Full Body C — Single-Leg, Press Variation and Lat Intent

Required primary roles:

* unilateral or single-leg lower-body pattern,
* horizontal press or push variation distinct from Day A,
* lat-biased pulling intent.

When high-anchor capability exists:

* include a true vertical pull where safe and appropriate.

Without high-anchor or pull-up-bar capability:

* preserve honest lat-biased work,
* do not falsely claim true vertical pulling,
* and record the capability limitation.

The session must remain a full-body workout rather than a band-accessory collection.

## 6. Equipment dominance and coherence

Mixed home should not mean every session uses every tool.

Use an explicit equipment-dominance policy.

Recommended targets:

* dumbbells provide most primary strength anchors;
* bands provide selected role advantages;
* bodyweight provides trunk, support and progression where appropriate.

Audit each session for:

* number of dumbbell exercises,
* number of band exercises,
* number of bodyweight exercises,
* reason each band exercise was selected,
* equipment transitions,
* anchor transitions,
* and whether the session remains coherent.

Flag sessions where:

* bands displace most dumbbell strength anchors without justification,
* every exercise changes equipment,
* the same role is duplicated across tools,
* or the session appears randomly assembled.

## 7. Setup-transition contract

Mixed-home workouts can become frustrating if equipment changes constantly.

Define and audit:

* dumbbell setup transitions,
* band setup transitions,
* anchor-height transitions,
* floor-to-standing transitions,
* and support-surface transitions.

Recommended limits:

### Beginner

* no more than three major equipment/setup blocks,
* no more than one anchor-height change where practical,
* minimal switching between dumbbells and bands.

### Intermediate

* no more than four major setup blocks,
* no more than two anchor-height changes,
* related exercises grouped together.

### Advanced

* complexity may increase only where the training value is clear,
* no more than two anchor-height changes,
* no high → low → high anchor sequence unless no safe alternative exists.

A major setup block is a contiguous group sharing the same primary equipment and setup.

The audit must report the setup sequence for every flagship session.

Do not sacrifice safe exercise ordering merely to reduce transitions.

## 8. Honest pulling

Mixed home must use the best confirmed pulling capability.

Required rules:

* high-anchor band pulldown may satisfy true vertical pulling;
* confirmed pull-up-bar exercise may satisfy true vertical pulling;
* one-arm dumbbell row may satisfy true horizontal pulling;
* valid middle-anchor row may satisfy true horizontal pulling;
* dumbbell pullover remains lat-biased or surrogate, not automatically true vertical pulling;
* pull-aparts, face pulls, sweeps, pulses and scapular drills cannot satisfy the only meaningful pulling role;
* mini loops cannot satisfy loaded upper-body pulling;
* no-anchor long bands cannot create fixed-anchor vertical pulling.

When no true vertical-pull capability exists, report the limitation honestly rather than forcing a false pass.

## 9. Exercise-selection priority

For every slot, selection should follow this priority:

1. satisfy the truthful movement role,
2. obey pain and safety requirements,
3. use confirmed equipment only,
4. preserve the session identity,
5. prefer the tool that provides the clearest training value,
6. minimize setup complexity,
7. preserve progression continuity,
8. avoid duplicate movement families.

Do not choose an exercise simply to increase equipment variety.

Add decision trace or audit output showing why band versus dumbbell was selected for important mixed-home slots.

## 10. Experience-based volume

Excluding warmup and finish work:

### Beginner

* three primary anchors,
* one or two support exercises,
* no more than five Build/Reinforce exercises,
* dumbbells should normally provide at least two primary strength anchors,
* no unnecessary equipment switching.

### Intermediate

* three or four primary anchors,
* up to two support exercises,
* no more than six Build/Reinforce exercises,
* one or two justified band advantages.

### Advanced

* four or five primary or secondary anchors,
* up to two support exercises,
* no more than seven Build/Reinforce exercises,
* extra volume must have a distinct role,
* additional equipment complexity must produce clear training value.

Do not add volume merely because more equipment is available.

## 11. Supported frequencies

Audit every mixed-home frequency currently supported.

### Three days

Use the flagship Full Body A/B/C architecture.

### Two days, if supported

Use two balanced full-body sessions and rotate omitted emphasis across weeks or phases.

### Four and five days

Extend the A/B/C family deliberately.

Additional sessions should:

* distribute movement stress,
* avoid repeating identical workouts,
* avoid heavy exposure to the same pattern on consecutive days,
* remain lower-volume where appropriate,
* preserve weekly squat, hinge, unilateral, push, horizontal pull, vertical pull when available, trunk and scapular coverage,
* control weekly equipment transitions,
* and avoid becoming isolated band-corrective days.

A fourth or fifth day may have a focused identity, but it must remain recognizably connected to the same mixed-home program.

Document the weekly rotation before implementing it.

## 12. Progression

Mixed-home progression should use the best progression variable available for each exercise.

Dumbbell progression may include:

* repetitions,
* load when available,
* tempo,
* pauses,
* range,
* unilateral difficulty,
* sets,
* harder variation.

Band progression may include:

* repetitions,
* stronger band where known,
* safe shortening,
* greater distance from anchor,
* tempo,
* pauses,
* range,
* unilateral difficulty,
* sets,
* harder variation.

Do not progress multiple demand variables simultaneously without explicit policy.

Do not assume band colour represents a standardized resistance.

Do not rotate from dumbbell to band merely to create novelty.

Preserve recognizable movement families across phases.

